<!-- transition: cube -->
## The Emacs model

| Part | Job |
|---|---|
| Buffers | durable application state |
| Windows | compose views |
| Commands | one action surface for people and agents |
| Modes | behavior in context |
| Lisp | the whole environment, live and open |

---

<!-- transition: flip; class: center -->
## The one rule

**Elixir supplies mechanism. Scheme decides policy.** {pop}

About 117,000 lines of Scheme and more than 1,000 commands, all redefinable while the editor runs {step up}

---

<!-- transition: blur; class: cols -->
## Why the BEAM

```text
buffer  -> GenServer
agent   -> supervised process
client  -> browser, shell, nc, JSON-RPC
```

- A blocking tool call freezes only its own process
- Every edit carries provenance: user, editor, process or agent
- Agents do not trigger themselves

---

<!-- transition: wipe; focus: on; step: left -->
## AI in every buffer

+ Chat is where the point is
+ Companion chats per buffer or per group
+ Direct API, ACP or codex-app-server
+ Agents write small Scheme programs, not slow tool round trips

---

<!-- transition: iris; class: center; bg: linear-gradient(135deg, #1f2a44, #3f7cac) -->
## Applications in userland Scheme

org-mode, spreadsheets, mail, git, dired, ibuffer, MCP hub, GraphQL, code browser, secrets, slides {up}

The next one is one short chat away {step fade}

---

<!-- transition: slide; class: cols -->
## Run it

```sh
mix deps.get
mix run --no-halt
open http://localhost:4004
```

- `C-x C-f` finds a file
- `C-x b` switches buffers
- `M-x` runs a command
- `M-:` evaluates Scheme

